# FS-UAE Launcher

This is the README file for FS-UAE Launcher.

## Installing FS-UAE Launcher

If you are using the source distribution, you should be able to install
FS-UAE Launcher by running "make install". The setup procedure
requires Python 3.12+ and setuptools to be installed. The tracked `bootstrap`
script generates the legacy setup file; do not edit the generated file.

## Python And Platform Target

The target is Python 3.12+ on Ubuntu 24.04+ and macOS. Pipenv's default
development interpreter is Python 3.12; package metadata has no upper bound.
Windows packaging is outside this support target and its legacy recipes are
not validation evidence.

CI is configured to test and build Python 3.12 and 3.15 independently on
Ubuntu 24.04 and macOS 15 Intel, with version-specific artifact names.
Configured jobs are not passed jobs: Python 3.15 support is pending successful
dependency installation, tests, and frozen platform builds on both systems.
Neither the new CI matrix nor macOS builds have been run in this local pass.
Python 3.13/3.14 are not separately validated by this matrix; macOS ARM builds
also remain unverified. Release publishing continues to select Python 3.12
artifacts; Python 3.15 artifacts are for validation, not an implied promotion.

For a locked source environment, install Pipenv under Python 3.12 and run:

  pipenv --python 3.12
  pipenv sync --dev
  pipenv run python fs-uae-launcher

To validate another matrix interpreter, explicitly create a separate Pipenv
environment with `pipenv --python /path/to/python3.15` before syncing. The
manifest's 3.12 value is the development default, not a package upper bound.
This stack's lock regeneration adds Ruff and interpreter metadata without
upgrading any existing locked dependency versions.

For a side-by-side Python 3.15 installation on Ubuntu without changing the
system Python, see [the isolated test environment instructions](docs/compiling.md#side-by-side-python-315-on-ubuntu).
Use a fresh environment for each Python minor version; an interpreter install
alone does not establish launcher compatibility.
The local Python 3.14.4 test environment is named `venv-3.14.4`; use
`source venv-3.14.4/bin/activate` or its interpreter explicitly to distinguish
it from other test versions. This environment name does not imply validated
Python 3.14 support.
The local Python 3.15 test environment is named `venv-3.15` (currently Python
3.15.1); activate it with `source venv-3.15/bin/activate`. Both versioned
environment directories are excluded from Git.
Local Ubuntu checks on Python 3.15.1 installed the existing locked dependencies,
passed 10 focused Python compatibility tests (one subprocess ResourceWarning),
and initialized Qt 6.12 offscreen. Full-suite, native desktop, and frozen-build
validation on 3.15 remain pending.

## Requirements

FS-UAE Launcher uses Qt 6 through `PySide6` (its essential modules are
enough), the `QtPy` compatibility layer, and `QtAwesome` for scalable
dashboard icons; `lhafile` reads `.lha` archives. Install the latest
releases supported by your Python version:

  python -m pip install --upgrade PySide6-Essentials QtPy QtAwesome requests lhafile

The Pipfile also declares these UI dependencies for Pipenv installations.
An existing virtual environment can be updated in place when its Python
minor version has not changed:

  venv/bin/python -m pip install --upgrade PySide6-Essentials QtPy QtAwesome requests lhafile

After an Ubuntu upgrade changes the Python minor version, create a fresh
environment instead. An old venv can follow the system Python symlink to the
new interpreter while its packages remain under the old version's directory,
causing errors such as `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'qtpy'`.
Keep the old environment until the replacement has been verified:

  python3 -m venv .venv
  .venv/bin/python -m pip install PySide6-Essentials QtPy QtAwesome requests lhafile
  .venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher

If venv creation reports that ensurepip is unavailable, install Ubuntu's
`python3-venv` package first. Use the new environment's Python explicitly
when launching, including in any desktop shortcut. This repairs dependency
installation; Python 3.14 remains outside the separately validated CI matrix.

The UI selects PySide6 by default. Use a clean process without another Qt
binding already imported; PyQt5 and PySide6 must not be mixed. Qt 6 handles
high-DPI scaling automatically. Supported saved themes are preserved; retired
theme names fall back to plain Fusion. Emulator, database, and networking
behavior is unchanged.

It can optionally use the `lhafile` python package to support .lha archives.
The old Windows Python 3.6/PyQt5/cx_Freeze instructions and unused Qt 5
bundle-cleaning scripts are retired. Supported Ubuntu/macOS packaging uses
Qt 6/PySide6 through `fsbuild/fix-qt.py` and `fsbuild/build.sh`; Windows
packaging remains unsupported. Do not mix Qt bindings in one environment.
Use the locked source environment above and the platform workflows described
in docs/compiling.md. Packaging policy checks run with:

  pipenv run python -m pytest fsbuild/tests/test_python_support.py

See [the Python and dependency modernization TODO](docs/python-dependency-modernization.md)
for the reviewed packaging, supported-Python, dependency, and code-quality
work items. For a map of the packages, startup sequence, theme engine and
data stores, see [docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md).

## Game Monitor Selection

Quick Settings selects a monitor for the loaded game. This per-game choice is
stored in the launcher's `Databases/GameOverrides.sqlite` database and follows
database variants or
configuration-file paths when switching games. Settings -> Video controls the
global default monitor used when a game has no monitor value or override.
Settings -> FS-UAE Launcher -> Window Placement can separately prefer a
display for the launcher window itself.

The dropdown lists detected display names, model names when available,
resolutions, and desktop coordinates. The default is the system primary display.
Hovering over a dropdown entry shows a compact monitor-layout diagram inside
the launcher, outlining that display and showing its name without changing the
saved selection. Selecting an entry keeps the preview visible briefly.
Cancelling the dropdown clears it. The preview is click-through, cannot take
keyboard focus, and never creates a fullscreen overlay or hides the desktop.
Explicit selections are saved using display identity rather than a left/right
guess or enumeration number. Window placement supports reordered and vertically
stacked displays. A disconnected saved display falls back to the primary display
without losing the saved choice. Reconnect it to use it again.
When available, a unique manufacturer/model/serial match follows a monitor
even when its connector name changes; displays without serials use their
existing connector identity. A per-game override takes precedence over the
game configuration, which takes precedence over the global monitor default.
An override can also request the emulator's own display default, and can be
cleared to return to the game's configuration/default.
Scale, stretch, bezel, zoom, Video Sync, and configured extra Quick Settings
use the same per-game override store. Video settings remain global defaults;
fullscreen in Quick Settings is explicitly a global default control.

Quick Settings are unavailable when there is no loaded game with a stable
identity. Unmatched archives and raw floppy/CD image launches do not have a
persistent per-game identity and therefore do not store Quick Settings
overrides.

FS-UAE's initial window position is now centred within the selected display,
not the launcher window. For fullscreen, the launcher also translates the
selection into FS-UAE 3.x's native monitor argument: this reliably represents
up to four displays with distinct horizontal positions. Stacked displays and
some positions in larger layouts cannot be represented by that older emulator
interface; choosing one shows a warning. Full support for those fullscreen
layouts requires emulator changes. Existing positional settings remain readable
as legacy choices; choose a detected display to replace them. Saved display
identity stays launcher-local rather than being written into emulator config.
Window placement ultimately remains subject to emulator and desktop/compositor
behaviour, especially Wayland and mixed-DPI setups.

## Deleting Saved Configurations

Local saved configurations have a trash icon at the right of their library row.
Click it, or select a configuration and press Delete, then confirm the file path.
Deletion is permanent and removes only the .fs-uae file and its index records;
game disks, ROMs, and save data are not removed. Database game entries cannot
be deleted this way. An open deleted configuration returns to the default
configuration, discarding its unsaved changes. Cancel leaves everything intact.
Deleting a different row preserves the currently loaded configuration and edits.
Finish any active database scan before deleting configurations.
Deletion keeps a private recovery copy until both indexes commit. A failed
commit restores the configuration and its index records; if file restoration
also fails, the recovery copy's location is reported in the launcher log.

## CPU Performance (Linux)

Open Settings -> FS-UAE to see the current CPU governor. Set Performance asks
for confirmation, then uses your desktop's PolicyKit authentication dialog to
run cpupower. The launcher never asks for, stores, or handles your password,
does not run as root, and does not change boot settings. No terminal sudo
command is needed. The desktop must have a working PolicyKit authentication
agent; system-installed cpupower and pkexec must be available. Utilities found
in user-owned PATH locations are not elevated. Missing or unsupported controls
are reported in Settings rather than attempted silently.

Changes affect the entire computer, not just FS-UAE, and can increase power
use, heat, and fan noise. Restore Previous restores the governor recorded for
each CPU policy before the change, including mixed-governor systems. Keep the
Settings window open to retain this restore snapshot: it is not saved across
launcher sessions, and closing the launcher does not automatically restore it.
Another system service or a reboot can change the governor. Refresh reads the
current state again; the launcher verifies the result of each completed change.

GameMode remains available and enabled by default for emulator-managed
optimizations while a game runs. A powersave governor is not proof of reduced
performance with modern Intel/AMD frequency drivers: the system performance
power profile can legitimately coexist with that governor. The explicit
governor control is separate from system power profiles and GameMode.

The tracked-source regression check includes extensionless Python scripts and
rejects literal `.format()` calls that Ruff leaves unchanged, including calls
with joins or dictionary lookups. The bootstrap uses f-strings while retaining
its generated setup and Makefile contents.
Output tests cover lazy WHDLoad logging, packaging templates,
and formatting after translation. Run these
checks and strict source compilation with:

  pipenv run python -m pytest fsbuild/tests/test_python_support.py

Simple synchronous command helpers use argument lists and `subprocess.run()`;
their existing output decoding and failure handling are retained. Streaming
archive readers, persistent emulator/server processes, and shell commands that
rely on expansion or quoting retain their existing process APIs. Command
regressions can be run with `pipenv run python -m pytest fsbuild/tests`.

Compatibility code uses supported executable lookup, timezone-aware UTC,
thread accessors, and importlib plugin loading. Locale discovery preserves
environment precedence without changing the process locale. Legacy Amiga
scan progress uses `process_time()`, retaining POSIX `time.clock()` CPU-time
semantics. Invalid-escape fixes preserve the original string values.

  QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen pipenv run python -m pytest launcher/tests/test_python_compatibility.py

Run the core regression slice with:

  venv/bin/python -m pytest fsbc fstd fspy launcher/netplay launcher/server/tests

The API layer resolves the three pre-existing invalid-escape warnings; strict
tracked Python compilation and the scoped Ruff gate pass on local Python 3.12.

The completed stack's local Python 3.12 validation passed 138 core, API,
command and packaging tests (10 skipped), plus 39 offscreen Qt tests and
65 subtests. This does not certify frozen builds or another platform/version.

## Empty-State Guidance

An empty library no longer shows a blank box. `launcher/ui/empty_state.py`
overlays "No games yet — Scan a folder with your Amiga disks and games" with a
"Scan a folder…" button that opens the Update File Database dialog. When a
search has no hits it says "Nothing matches your search" and offers "Clear
search" instead. The overlay follows the list's model, so it hides as soon as
rows appear. Floppy, CD, tape and cartridge slots show placeholder text such
as "No disk inserted — click the folder to choose one" when empty.

## Play Tile

The Overview workspace opens with a Play card above the library: the
selected game's name, a one-line hint about where it came from, a large Play
button (the same action as Start / Launch Amiga, disabled while a game is
running) and an "Edit configuration" toggle. The detailed configuration editor
is hidden until that toggle is on; choosing any configuration page from the
navigation rail also reveals it. The choice is remembered in
`launcher_show_editor`. Implementation: `launcher/ui/play_tile.py`.

## Plain-Language Model Names

The Amiga Model selector on the Overview page shows everyday names such as
"Amiga 500" and "Amiga 1200", and its variant list describes what differs
("Standard (1 MB total memory)", "Blizzard 1230 accelerator") instead of
"1.3 ROM, 512 KB". Hovering an entry shows the original technical title and
subtitle, and the Accuracy and NTSC controls explain what they do. The
mapping lives in `launcher/ui/config/model_labels.py`; stored configuration
values (`amiga_model=A1200/1230`) are unchanged.

## Modern Dark Workspace

ROM & memory, Expansions, and Advanced use aligned label columns and evenly
expanding value controls, following Overview's form layout. ROM picker rows
share label and selector widths; memory options use the same single-column
layout as the other forms, keeping controls usable in narrower workspaces.
This is presentation-only: defaults, overrides, and configuration values are
unchanged across all four workspace themes.

Form regression tests check all four themes at 540, 900, and 1500 pixel form
widths, including ROM-row alignment and override/value interactions.

Settings pages share aligned label/warning columns and expanding text fields,
dropdowns, and numeric inputs, with help buttons kept at the right edge.
Configuration override labels have extra spacing before their value fields,
including the long Empty Floppy Drive Volume label. Setting values, defaults,
and compact quick-setting rows retain their existing behavior.

Modern Dark is the default when no launcher theme has been selected. The
launcher opens maximized on its current display, with a 1080p-oriented
workspace, a labeled navigation rail, a searchable configuration library,
a resizable configuration editor, and persistent launch controls. All eight
configuration pages remain available. Settings, net play, tools, account
actions, and configuration warnings retain their existing behavior.

The library can be hidden with its toolbar toggle. Quick settings open in a
separate resizable pane, temporarily replacing the library below 1400 pixels
of window width. Screenshots and cover information use the selected
game's existing artwork; the media strip hides below 820 pixels of window
height so editing remains usable. The supported minimum window size is
1000 x 720. Long editor pages scroll rather than forcing a larger window.

Select Modern Dark in the Launcher settings and restart to replace a
previously saved theme. It can also be requested explicitly at startup:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-modern

The available themes are Modern Dark, four futuristic skins of it (Neon City,
Synthwave Sunset, Holo Grid, Phosphor Terminal), Fusion, Windows 3.11, and
Amiga Workbench 1.3. All share this single-window workspace and embedded
Net Play page. Fusion retains Qt's original light palette and
native-style controls. Removed or unknown saved themes fall back to Fusion; an
empty theme setting still defaults to Modern Dark. Retired saved theme names
are normalized to Fusion so the Settings picker agrees with the running theme.
QtAwesome is installed in the project venv; other source installations must
install it as above.

### Futuristic Skins

The skins reuse Modern Dark's layout, controls and tests; only the colour
palette, corner radius and (for Phosphor Terminal) the font differ. They are
defined as `ModernPalette` records in `fsui/qt/modern_theme.py`, and the
dashboard, Settings navigation, Net Play panel, monitor diagram and
QtAwesome icons read their colours from the active palette instead of
hard-coded values, so a new skin only needs a palette entry and an option
label.

| Setting value       | Name               | Look                                              |
|---------------------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `fusion-modern`     | Modern Dark        | Slate navy with cyan accent (default)             |
| `fusion-cyberpunk`  | Neon City          | Near-black, magenta accent, cyan glow, sharp edges|
| `fusion-synthwave`  | Synthwave Sunset   | Deep violet, orange accent, pink highlights       |
| `fusion-hologram`   | Holo Grid          | Dark teal, electric cyan, square corners          |
| `fusion-terminal`   | Phosphor Terminal  | Green-on-black monospace terminal                 |

Pick one in Settings > FS-UAE Launcher > Launcher Theme and click Restart
Launcher, or try one for a single run:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-cyberpunk

Plain Fusion can also be selected for a single launch:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-plain

Settings uses the same spacious navigation rows, scalable icons, and cyan
selection accent as the main workspace. Its navigation scrolls when needed.
Modern Dark also applies flat, rounded buttons, dropdowns with custom chevrons,
clear keyboard-focus outlines, and larger checkbox/radio indicators throughout
the launcher. These are native Qt controls; no browser or React runtime is
required. The other three themes use their own palette and controls.
Help and netplay information buttons and status-bar notices use the same cyan
icon family as the navigation rail in Modern Dark.

### Quick Theme Switching
Choosing a theme or interface size in Settings > FS-UAE Launcher applies it to
the running launcher immediately: the application palette and stylesheet,
the dashboard, Settings navigation and Net Play panel all re-render
(`fsui.qt.qt.apply_launcher_theme` notifies `theme_applied` listeners).
The interface size is a Smaller / Default / Larger slider
(`launcher/settings/ui_scale.py`) with a "Use Default Size" button; it still
stores a point size in `launcher_font_size`, so existing preferences and
Reset to Defaults behave as before. Dragging previews on release.
Default size follows the selected theme's unoverridden font, rather than
assuming the desktop's font size. "Use Default Size" clears a stored override,
even when it already equals the default. Font-only changes preserve an active
command-line theme; existing controls, label columns, navigation rows and
scroll-area minimum sizes are remeasured without a manual window resize.
Restart Launcher remains available for a full restart. After confirmation,
preferences are saved and the launcher
reopens with the selected theme and font. The current base directory is
preserved; old theme overrides and game/disk command-line arguments are not
repeated. Active netplay games and the lobby on this computer are stopped. Save
configuration edits before restarting. Cancelling leaves the launcher open.

### GUI Cleanup

The obsolete standalone netplay/server GUI wrappers and the launcher's
old theme-specific startup, drawing, and resize fallbacks have been removed.
Theme choices now come from one option definition. The tab/book widgets remain
in use by configuration-page construction; shared toolkit window classes and
independently accessible tools are not treated as dead code. Emulator, server
process, database, and network protocol logic is outside this GUI cleanup.

### Retro Themes

The Launcher theme setting also offers Windows 3.11 and Amiga Workbench 1.3.
Select a theme and restart the launcher. Both use the single-window workspace:
Windows 3.11 has gray beveled controls, white fields, and navy selections;
Workbench has a blue background, white square controls, orange selections,
and a monospace font. Fonts fall back to installed alternatives. These are
inspired styles, not emulations; outer window decorations remain controlled
by the operating system. They do not replace any emulator or netplay behavior.
Retro Settings opens at 1100 x 720 to accommodate its wider typography.
Retro navigation uses original, scalable tile treatments around familiar
QtAwesome symbols: teal-topped beveled windows for Windows 3.11 and folded
white tiles with blue/orange details for Workbench. They are modern
reinterpretations, not copies of the original operating systems' artwork.
Settings shares the same icon family, roomy navigation rows, square button
edges, and navy/orange selection colours as its theme's main sidebar. Page
headings use the same typography as the workspace, without large bitmap art.

They can also be selected for a single launch:

  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-windows311
  venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher --launcher-theme=fusion-workbench

### Integrated Netplay

In all four themes, Net Play is a page inside the main window rather than a
separate window. The navigation button and Tools menu open the same page.
Library and quick-settings tools are disabled on it.

The page is a lobby browser: it watches every lobby server in Settings > Net
Play, each on its own so a slow or unreachable server doesn't hold up the
rest, and lists every game waiting for players on them. Join takes a slot,
Join by ID... joins an unlisted game, and Host game hosts the loaded config
on a listed server or on This computer. A game starts by itself once every
player has joined and is ready. While in a game the page shows its players,
Re-check, Leave (End game for a host) and what the game is waiting for. An
activity log keeps the last 200 lines. Watching carries on while other pages
show. See docs/netplay-rooms-design.md.

Hosting on This computer runs fsnp-server as a lobby on the port in Settings >
Net Play (25101 by default), telling players the address set there. The
lobby is kept between games until Stop lobby, which won't stop a game being
played, or until the launcher closes. The server's bind result determines
whether the port is available; a busy port produces an explicit message.

## UI Regression Tests

Run the widget, dialog, menu, painting, and screen tests without a display:

    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen python -m unittest discover -s fsui/test -t .

Native file dialogs, multi-monitor placement, and platform-specific appearance
also need interactive testing on Linux, Windows, and macOS.
Image-button regression tests cover icon loading and activation, including
the launcher's New button, under PySide6.
Dashboard tests cover page routing, library/quick-settings toggles, the dark
palette, the theme picker, and 1920x1080, 1280x800, and 1000x720 layouts.
The navigation and embedded-netplay checks run in all four workspace themes,
including the page persisting between visits and shutdown on main close.
Settings tests cover navigation icons, page switching, and headings in all
four workspace themes, including the retro themes' larger icons,
palette-based sidebar styling, and matching workspace typography.
Icon-rendering tests cover theme differences and nonblank output at 20, 24,
and 48 pixels in normal, selected, and disabled states.
Control tests verify SVG assets, dropdown selection, checkbox activation,
and numeric stepping.
Theme checks cover saved and command-line retro selection, their palettes,
picker entries, and modern info-icon styling and help-link activation.
Fusion checks cover its light palette, exact four-theme picker, retired-theme
migration, and shared Settings/workspace/server routing.
Restart checks cover confirmation/cancellation, save-before-close ordering,
source/frozen commands, clean launch arguments, shutdown notifications,
and save/relaunch failure handling.
List-selection tests also verify that clearing a search selection emits None
rather than converting it to an incorrect row index under PySide6.
Launch regression tests exercise Start-button dispatch and construction of
the launch task dialog with and without a netplay session. Netplay tests
cover the lobby browser's servers, games, joining, hosting and game views
without opening a network connection or launching an emulator; the
launcher/netplay tests run the same flows against a real fsnp-server.
Status-bar text and warnings use the widget's live WindowText palette colour;
inactive text keeps its existing grey override. All four themes inherit their
palette backgrounds on every OS unless an explicit background override is set.
Palette regression tests exercise both status painters, inactive text, and
background selection with simulated Windows/macOS/Linux flags.

## GNOME Startup Compatibility

Some Qt/GNOME combinations abort during startup with a GLib error about the
missing `org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.xsettings` `antialiasing` key.
This is a desktop theme integration failure, not a missing Python package.
On Linux GNOME desktops the UI defaults to Qt's generic platform theme to
avoid that integration. Explicit `QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME` settings are respected;
other desktops and operating systems are unchanged. GNOME-specific theme and
file-dialog integration may differ with the generic platform theme.

To explicitly select the workaround when launching from a terminal:

  QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=generic venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher

## Running FS-UAE Launcher

You start FS-UAE Launcher by running the following command:

    $ fs-uae-launcher

You can also run FS-UAE Launcher without installation by running the
following command in the source root directory:

    $ ./fs-uae-launcher

For an existing virtual environment, explicitly use its Python interpreter:

    $ venv/bin/python fs-uae-launcher

